---
source: crates/splot-cli/tests/help_snapshots.rs
expression: "help(\"encode\")"
---
Encode a Y4M file to an AV2 IVF or Annex B stream

Usage: splot encode [OPTIONS] --output <OUTPUT> <INPUT>

Arguments:
  <INPUT>  Input Y4M file, or `-` for standard input

Options:
  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output file, in the container of `--output-format`
      --output-format <OUTPUT_FORMAT>
          Output container: `ivf`, or `annexb`, the raw length-delimited OBUs of AV2 Annex B without a container header [default: ivf] [possible values: ivf, annexb]
      --qp <QP>
          Constant quantizer (`base_q_idx`) for every frame: 1..=255 at 8 bits, 1..=303 at 10 bits; `--lossless` codes 0
      --lossless
          Lossless: every frame at `base_q_idx` 0, so the decoder output equals the input samples
      --crf <CRF>
          Constant quality with lookahead offsets: rate factor 1..=63, higher is smaller; the default mode, at 20
      --max-bitrate <KBPS>
          Maximum bitrate of constant quality (`--crf` or the default) in kilobits per second: a frame that would underflow the decoder buffer of `--buffer` is coded again more coarsely
      --bitrate <BITRATE>
          Target bitrate in kilobits per second
      --rc <RC>
          Bitrate mode for `--bitrate`: `vbr` (default) or `cbr` (decoder buffer of `--buffer`) [possible values: vbr, cbr]
      --buffer <MS>
          Decoder buffer of `--rc cbr` and `--max-bitrate`, in milliseconds at the bitrate [default: 1000]
      --initial-buffer <MS>
          Decoder buffer level before the first frame, in milliseconds at the bitrate [default: half of `--buffer`]
      --two-pass
          Two-pass VBR: a first pass at half size and constant qindex reads the input once more and sets each frame's share of the budget. Needs a file input
      --preset <PRESET>
          Speed preset: 0 is the slowest and best, 10 the fastest [default: 6]
      --preset-set <FIELD=VALUE>
          Overrides one field of the preset, as FIELD=VALUE; repeat for more fields. It wins over the screen-content switch. Flags take 0, 1, false or true; a per-frame-kind field (`intra_modes`, `tcq`, `tf_frames`, `tf_strength`) takes one value or four comma-separated values for key, base, mid and leaf frames; `mvd_tools` takes three comma-separated flags. An unknown FIELD lists the valid names
      --low-delay
          Low delay: one packet per input frame, in display order. Sets `mini_gop_size=1` and `bitrate_mini_gop_size=1` (IPPP, every frame shown) and `lookahead_frames=1` before `--preset-set`; off by default
      --tune <TUNE>
          The metric the encoder tunes for: `psnr` (default), `ssim` (superblock lambda and qindex follow the luma variance) or `vq` (trials keep the AC energy of the source); before `--preset-set` [default: psnr] [possible values: psnr, ssim, vq]
      --fast-decode
          Fast decode: 64x64 superblocks, no Wiener-NS or GDF, and at most two CDEF strength sets, before `--preset-set`; off by default
      --multi-frame-header
          Multi-frame header: each key frame codes a multi-frame header OBU, and every frame header refers to it instead of the sequence header; off by default
      --threads <THREADS>
          Worker-thread policy: `auto` (default), a positive integer, or `0` (alias for auto) [default: auto]
      --tiles <TILES>
          Tile columns x rows, each a power of two up to 64. More tiles code faster on more threads and cost some compression [default: 1x1]
      --tile-widths <W1,W2,..>
          Tile column widths in superblocks of the sequence: the first columns of a non-uniform layout; one more column takes the rest of the frame. Needs `--tiles 1x1`
      --tile-heights <H1,H2,..>
          Tile row heights in superblocks of the sequence, as `--tile-widths`
      --tile-groups <N>
          Tile groups per frame: the tiles split in raster order into N tile group OBUs; N above the tile count codes one tile per group [default: 1]
      --limit <LIMIT>
          Stop after this many input frames
      --resize-denominator <D>
          Fixed-ratio resize: codes every frame at 8/D of the input size per axis, D in 9..=16; off by default. The IVF, its decode and the `--stats` PSNR (against the resized source) are at the coded size
      --crop <L,R,T,B>
          Cropping window: cut L, R, T and B luma samples off the left, right, top and bottom of the coded frame (after --resize-denominator); the stream decodes to the rest. L and T fall on a chroma sample (even for 4:2:0, L even for 4:2:2). Off by default. The `--stats` PSNR covers the full coded frame
      --film-grain
          Film grain: each key frame codes a grain model fitted to the noise of its source, and every output frame applies it at decode; off by default. The encoder does not denoise, so the stats PSNR does not include the grain
      --cbr-padding
          CBR filler: a temporal unit that would overflow the decoder buffer ends with a padding OBU of the overflow, so the stream keeps the target bitrate. Only with `--rc cbr`; off by default
      --color-primaries <N>
          H.273 colour primaries code point (1 BT.709, 9 BT.2020), written to the stream as declared; the samples are not converted. Without the colour flags the stream declares no colour
      --transfer-characteristics <N>
          H.273 transfer characteristics code point (1 BT.709, 16 PQ, 18 HLG)
      --matrix-coefficients <N>
          H.273 matrix coefficients code point (1 BT.709, 9 BT.2020 NCL)
      --color-range <COLOR_RANGE>
          Sample range; the default is the Y4M `XCOLORRANGE`, else limited [possible values: limited, full]
      --chroma-sample-position <CHROMA_SAMPLE_POSITION>
          Chroma sample position of 4:2:0 or 4:2:2 input; the default is the one of a `C420mpeg2` (left) or `C420paldv` (top-left) Y4M header [possible values: left, center, top-left, top, bottom-left, bottom]
      --sar <W:H>
          Sample aspect ratio; the default is the Y4M `A` tag
      --timing-info
          Write the frame rate to the stream as timing info; off by default
      --mastering-display <G(x,y)B(x,y)R(x,y)WP(x,y)L(max,min)>
          HDR mastering display as `G(x,y)B(x,y)R(x,y)WP(x,y)L(max,min)`: chromaticities in units of 0.00002, luminance in units of 0.0001 cd/m2 (the SMPTE ST 2086 integers)
      --content-light <CLL,FALL>
          HDR content light level, MaxCLL and MaxFALL in cd/m2
      --icc <FILE>
          ICC profile file (ISO 15076-1, at least its 128-byte header): every key frame carries its bytes unchanged in an ICC profile metadata OBU
      --itut-t35 <FILE>
          ITU-T T.35 file: byte 0 is the country code, byte 1 the extension byte when the code is 0xFF, then the payload (at least one byte, the terminal provider code first). Every key frame carries it unchanged in a T.35 metadata OBU
      --user-data-unregistered <FILE>
          Unregistered user data file: a 16-byte UUID, then the payload. Every key frame carries it unchanged in a user data metadata OBU
      --timecode
          Every output frame carries a full timecode metadata OBU, counted from 00:00:00 frame 0 at the input frame rate, without dropped counts
      --temporal-point-info
          Every output frame carries a temporal point info metadata OBU: its presentation time in frames since the last key frame. Needs `--timing-info`
      --scan-type-info
          Every output frame carries a scan type metadata OBU: a progressive frame, not a duplicate. Every key frame then writes a content interpretation OBU
      --banding-hints <FILE>
          Banding hints file: the bytes of one metadata_banding_hints() (AV2 § 5.17.8), padded to a byte. Every output frame carries it in a banding hints metadata OBU. The encoder does not detect banding
      --keyint <KEYINT>
          Distance between key frames, in frames [default: 120]
      --stats
          Print `frame,pts,qp,bits,psnr_y,psnr_u,psnr_v` CSV lines to stdout
  -h, --help
          Print help
  -V, --version
          Print version
